#version 450

// Sustained load on both the ALU pipes and the memory subsystem, heavy enough
// that the temperature and power numbers on the certificate mean something.
//
// Two things were wrong with the original kernel, found in that order.
//
// 1. It was one serial dependency chain.
//
//        a = fma(a, b, k);   // b's next value needs this result
//        b = fma(b, a, k);   // a's next value needs this one
//        a = a - floor(a * 0.001) * 1000.0;
//
//    Every operation depended on the one before it, so each thread had at most
//    one FMA in flight and spent the rest of the time on latency that occupancy
//    could not hide. The floor() calls made it worse: special-function work is
//    issued on a much narrower unit than the FMA pipe, so the bounding
//    arithmetic throttled the thing being measured while contributing nothing.
//
//    On an RTX 3070 that produced 149 W on a 220 W card and 60 C, while NVML
//    reported 98% utilization. Utilization is the misleading number: it is the
//    fraction of time a kernel was resident, not how much of the die was
//    working, so a kernel leaving most of the FMA units idle still reads 98%.
//
// 2. Its working set was 4 MiB, which fits in cache on every card worth
//    certifying. An RTX 3070 has 4 MB of L2 and an RX 6600 has 32 MB of
//    Infinity Cache, so after the first pass the kernel never touched VRAM
//    again. The memory subsystem, on the card whose memory this product exists
//    to certify, sat idle through the entire thermal test. Board power is core
//    plus memory plus the rest, so a load that skips memory cannot approach the
//    card's rated draw no matter how well the ALUs are fed.
//
// So: eight independent FMA chains for the ALU side, and a streaming read and
// write over a buffer far larger than any cache for the memory side.
//
// The chains never read each other, so the compiler issues them back to back
// and the FMA pipeline stays fed. Bounding is free, because each chain is
// x = fma(x, m, c) with m just under 1.0, which converges toward c/(1-m) and
// can neither overflow nor produce a NaN, so there are no clamping
// instructions in the loop at all.
//
// The streamed access is one load and one store per block of 128 FMAs, not per
// FMA. Per-FMA would make this a bandwidth test: at one access per iteration a
// dispatch spends roughly ten times longer waiting on memory than computing,
// which would starve the ALUs and measure the wrong thing. One per block puts
// the two within the same order of magnitude on every card tested, so both
// subsystems are working.
//
// Consecutive threads touch consecutive addresses, so accesses coalesce, and
// the offset advances by the whole thread count each block, so the kernel walks
// the buffer linearly instead of sitting in one cache line. The load's latency
// is hidden by the FMAs issued after it.
//
// Multipliers come from the buffer's contents so none of this folds at compile
// time, and the results are written back so none of it is dead.
//
// Note for anyone tempted to switch API: this being Vulkan is not why the old
// kernel ran cool, and OpenGL or D3D12 would not have run hotter. FurMark heats
// cards through what it computes per pixel, not through the API it computes it
// in. The instruction mix and the working set are the whole story.

layout(local_size_x = 256) in;

layout(std430, binding = 0) buffer Data {
    float values[];
};

// Sized at runtime to be far larger than any cache. See stress.rs.
layout(std430, binding = 1) buffer Stream {
    float stream[];
};

layout(push_constant) uniform Push {
    uint blocks;
    // Element count minus one. A power of two, so wrapping is a mask rather
    // than a modulo, which would be an integer divide in the inner loop.
    uint stream_mask;
    // Total threads in the dispatch, so each block moves the whole grid one
    // stride further through the buffer and the walk stays coalesced.
    uint stride;
} pc;

void main() {
    uint idx = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
    if (idx >= values.length()) {
        return;
    }

    // Runtime-dependent, so nothing below folds to a constant.
    float seed = fract(values[idx] * 0.0001 + float(idx) * 0.6180339887);

    // Named scalars rather than an array, so they are guaranteed to live in
    // registers instead of risking a spill to local memory, which would turn
    // the ALU half of this into an accidental memory test.
    float a0 = seed + 0.10;
    float a1 = seed + 0.20;
    float a2 = seed + 0.30;
    float a3 = seed + 0.40;
    float a4 = seed + 0.50;
    float a5 = seed + 0.60;
    float a6 = seed + 0.70;
    float a7 = seed + 0.80;

    // Just under 1.0, so each chain converges instead of diverging.
    float m = 0.99900 + seed * 0.00001;
    float c = 0.00012345;

    float streamed = 0.0;
    uint offset = idx & pc.stream_mask;

    for (uint b = 0u; b < pc.blocks; b++) {
        // Issued first so its latency is covered by the arithmetic below.
        float s = stream[offset];

        for (uint i = 0u; i < 8u; i++) {
            a0 = fma(a0, m, c);
            a1 = fma(a1, m, c);
            a2 = fma(a2, m, c);
            a3 = fma(a3, m, c);
            a4 = fma(a4, m, c);
            a5 = fma(a5, m, c);
            a6 = fma(a6, m, c);
            a7 = fma(a7, m, c);

            a0 = fma(a0, m, c);
            a1 = fma(a1, m, c);
            a2 = fma(a2, m, c);
            a3 = fma(a3, m, c);
            a4 = fma(a4, m, c);
            a5 = fma(a5, m, c);
            a6 = fma(a6, m, c);
            a7 = fma(a7, m, c);
        }

        // Writing the loaded value back plus a live accumulator keeps the store
        // real, keeps the load from being optimised away, and cannot make the
        // buffer diverge: a0 is bounded and s is whatever was there before.
        stream[offset] = fma(s, 0.5, a0 * 0.5);
        streamed += s;

        offset = (offset + pc.stride) & pc.stream_mask;
    }

    // Written back so none of the above is dead code.
    values[idx] = ((a0 + a1) + (a2 + a3)) + ((a4 + a5) + (a6 + a7)) + streamed * 0.0001;
}
